---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Coverage types explained"
  description="Plain-language explanations of common car and home insurance coverage types."
>
  <a class="back-link" href="/learn/">&larr; All guides</a>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Coverage types explained</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Insurance policies bundle several distinct coverages together. Knowing
      what each one actually pays for makes it much easier to compare two
      quotes fairly — a lower price is often a narrower policy, not a
      discount on the same thing.
    </p>

    <h2>Auto insurance</h2>

    <h3>Liability coverage</h3>
    <p>
      Pays for injuries or property damage you cause to <em>others</em> in an
      accident you're responsible for. It's typically split into bodily
      injury liability (per person and per accident) and property damage
      liability. Every state we've verified requires some minimum amount —
      see the <a href="/auto-insurance/">state pages</a> for the actual
      figures.
    </p>

    <h3>Collision</h3>
    <p>
      Pays to repair or replace <em>your own</em> vehicle after a collision,
      regardless of fault, up to your vehicle's value minus your deductible.
    </p>

    <h3>Comprehensive</h3>
    <p>
      Pays for damage to your own vehicle from causes other than a collision
      — theft, fire, vandalism, weather, and hitting an animal are common
      examples.
    </p>

    <h3>Uninsured / underinsured motorist</h3>
    <p>
      Pays for your injuries (and sometimes property damage) when the at-fault
      driver has no insurance, or not enough. Some states require it; in
      others it's optional or must be offered but can be declined.
    </p>

    <h3>PIP, no-fault, and medical payments</h3>
    <p>
      Personal Injury Protection (PIP) and "no-fault" coverage pay your own
      medical costs regardless of who caused the accident, and are required
      in some states. Medical payments coverage is a narrower, optional
      alternative in states that don't require PIP.
    </p>

    <h3>Other common add-ons</h3>
    <p>
      Rental reimbursement, roadside assistance, and gap coverage (which
      covers the difference between what you owe on a loan/lease and the
      car's value) are optional coverages that some drivers add on top of the
      core policy.
    </p>

    <h2>Home insurance</h2>

    <h3>Dwelling coverage</h3>
    <p>
      Pays to repair or rebuild the structure of your home after a covered
      loss. This limit should reflect what it would actually cost to rebuild
      — not the home's market value or purchase price.
    </p>

    <h3>Other structures</h3>
    <p>
      Covers structures on the property that aren't the main house — a
      detached garage, fence, or shed, for example.
    </p>

    <h3>Personal property</h3>
    <p>
      Covers your belongings inside the home. High-value items like jewelry
      or firearms often have special limits and may need to be separately
      scheduled.
    </p>

    <h3>Loss of use</h3>
    <p>
      Pays reasonable additional living expenses if a covered loss makes your
      home temporarily unlivable.
    </p>

    <h3>Personal liability and medical payments</h3>
    <p>
      Personal liability covers you if someone is injured on your property
      (or you're otherwise found responsible for injury or property damage)
      and sues. Medical payments coverage pays small medical bills for a
      guest injured on your property, regardless of fault.
    </p>

    <h3>Replacement cost vs. actual cash value</h3>
    <p>
      Replacement cost pays what it currently costs to repair or rebuild.
      Actual cash value pays that amount minus depreciation — meaning it
      pays less, especially for older items or an older roof.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        <strong>Flood and earthquake are usually separate policies.</strong>
        Standard homeowners insurance generally excludes both. Flood coverage
        is typically bought through the NFIP or a private flood insurer;
        earthquake coverage is a separate policy or endorsement. An umbrella
        policy can add liability coverage above your home and auto limits.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      To see which of these facts are verified for a specific state, visit
      the <a href="/auto-insurance/">auto</a> or
      <a href="/home-insurance/">home</a> state pages, or check your own
      numbers with the <a href="/tools/">Coverage Gap Checker</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
